OpenAI has confirmed that GPT-6 Astra is the first model it has broadly deployed to reach "Critical" status under its Preparedness Framework for cybersecurity. Under OpenAI's own criteria, a model hits the Critical threshold when it can identify and develop functional zero-day exploits of all severity levels across hardened real-world critical systems without human intervention, or devise and execute entirely new end-to-end attack strategies against hardened targets. OpenAI stated in its system card that Astra can find previously unknown security flaws and develop new exploitation methods across many well-protected systems without step-by-step human guidance.

In evaluation, OpenAI created a newer version of ExploitBench using vulnerabilities disclosed after Astra's knowledge cutoff. During testing, Astra discovered and leveraged previously unknown zero-day vulnerabilities as part of its exploit chains. OpenAI says it is in the process of disclosing two such vulnerabilities to the relevant maintainers. The company has also strengthened Astra's jailbreak resistance, isolation, checkpoint encryption, and monitoring, though it acknowledges the model is harder to supervise than previous generations.

This is significant because it marks the first time a commercially deployed AI model has met a rigorous, self-defined bar for autonomous offensive cyber capability. The dual-use implications are stark: the same capability that finds zero-days for defenders also creates new attack surface for adversaries. Organizations should track which AI models their vendors and partners integrate, and expect that AI-assisted vulnerability discovery will compress the window between disclosure and exploitation across the industry.
